Route the real Node extension host around cp.fork() on iOS.

iOS denies fork()/posix_spawn() to third-party processes outright, at the
sandbox-profile level -- unrelated to JIT or code signing, and with no known
workaround short of a full jailbreak. cp.fork() is exactly how this file
normally starts the extension host, so it can't run as-is.

The exthost protocol itself doesn't require a literal parent/child
relationship, though: on POSIX, when _canSendSocket is false, the only
thing that happens is a plain net.Server listens on a Unix-domain-socket
path (createRandomIPCHandle()) and whatever process connects to it is
treated as the extension host. This patch (a) forces _canSendSocket off
whenever IPADVSCODE_NO_FORK is set, so that path is always taken, and
(b) replaces the cp.fork() call with writing the launch parameters
(bootstrapForkPath/args/env) to a JSON file in the shared App Group
container instead. A second app extension (ExtensionHostRuntime, launched
by iOS itself, not by us) reads that file and execs bootstrap-fork.js on
its own, connecting to the same socket. See ipad-vscode/README.md and
ipad-vscode/Sources/ExtensionHostRuntime for the other half of this.

Known gap: since there's no real ChildProcess handle on this path,
shortenReconnectionGraceTimeIfNecessary() and the fd-passing branch of
acceptReconnection() are inert, and the exit/error lifecycle hooks don't
fire for this process the way they do for a real fork. Not addressed here.

Only takes effect when IPADVSCODE_NO_FORK is set; behavior on every other
platform is unchanged.

